# function Point() {}; Object.assign(Point.prototype, { norm: f, count: 1, get half() { .. } }); Object.assign(Point.prototype, { norm: f, scale: f }, { scale: g })
# Object.assign stores the values of the fields of its sources. A name under which the literals hold one function is listed with that function, as if the program had stored it under the name:
# a call under the name tests the bit of the class, which the run time sets where the prototype object holds that function. A name that may hold something else is listed without a function.
# What a getter of a source answers is not followed.
